"""Produce a readable, standalone research audit from computed results."""
import json,html
from pathlib import Path
D=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'docs/research/backtest'
legacy=json.loads((D/'public-benchmark-result.json').read_text());initial_modern=json.loads((D/'public-benchmark-modern-result.json').read_text());modern=json.loads((D/'public-benchmark-tolerance-result.json').read_text())
q=json.loads((D/'qualification-audit.json').read_text())
e=html.escape
money=lambda n:'${:,.0f}'.format(n)
pct=lambda n:'{:.1f}%'.format(100*n)
def headline(r):
 m=r['metrics'];return f"<tr><td>{r.get('holdoutYear',2020)} · {'±$50k tuning' if r.get('tuningObjective')=='within50000' else 'dollar MAE tuning'}</td><td>{m['n']}</td><td>{round(m['within50000']*m['n'])} / {m['n']} ({pct(m['within50000'])})</td><td>{money(m['medianAbsoluteError'])}</td><td>{money(m['meanAbsoluteError'])}</td></tr>"
def bands(r):
 return ''.join(f"<tr><td>{e(k)}</td><td>{m['n']}</td><td>{pct(m['within50000'])}</td><td>{money(m['medianAbsoluteError'])}</td></tr>" for k,m in r['byClosingPrice'].items() if m)
def cases(r):
 return ''.join(f"<tr><td><a href='{e(p['source'])}'>{e(p['id'])}</a></td><td>{money(p['actual'])}</td><td>{money(p['predicted'])}</td><td>{money(p['signedError'])}</td></tr>" for p in sorted(r['predictions'],key=lambda p:p['absoluteError'],reverse=True)[:10])
def baselines(r):
 rows=[('Learned model',r['metrics']),*r['baselines'].items()]
 return ''.join(f"<tr><td>{e(n)}</td><td>{pct(m['within50000'])}</td><td>{money(m['medianAbsoluteError'])}</td><td>{money(m['meanAbsoluteError'])}</td></tr>" for n,m in rows)
rows=''.join(f"<tr><td>{e(r['id'])}</td><td>{'Admitted for research' if r['eligible'] else 'Excluded'}</td><td>{e('; '.join(r['reasons']) or '; '.join(r['warnings']))}</td></tr>" for r in q['modes']['retrospective']['records'])
s=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Cornerpin — valuation qualification audit</title>
<style>body{{background:#101a17;color:#f3f1e9;font:16px/1.6 system-ui;margin:0}}main{{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:44px 24px}}h1{{font-size:38px;line-height:1.15}}h2{{margin-top:48px;color:#e2c79f}}a{{color:#edb77e}}.note{{border-left:4px solid #ff6a32;padding:16px 22px;background:#22322c}}table{{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:20px 0;font-size:14px}}td,th{{text-align:left;padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #415148}}th{{color:#e2c79f}}.scroll{{overflow:auto}}small{{color:#bdc8c2}}code{{overflow-wrap:anywhere}}@media print{{body{{background:white;color:black}}h2,th,a{{color:#243d32}}.note{{background:#f5f1e9}}}}</style>
<main><small>CORNERPIN · RESEARCH AUDIT · NOVEMBER 7, 2026</small><h1>The zero-record result was partly a rules problem. The accuracy problem remains.</h1>
<p class="note">Acceptance band: <b>±$50,000 per estimate</b>. Real public-sale backtests now run, but neither result supports a reliable production lot valuation. Both are external Cook County benchmarks, <b>not Utah validation</b>.</p>
<h2>1. All 17 pilot candidates, audited</h2><p><b>3</b> county-documented land sales are admitted for retrospective research. <b>13</b> lack attributable closing prices. <b>1</b> includes a house and other improvements. The three retained records are agricultural/development land with unresolved comparability and transaction details; none is certified as a finished residential-lot comparable.</p>
<p>The importer had erased supported classifications, required all 13 attributes, and put every priced transaction in training. Research admission is now distinct from model support. Unknown optional features stay missing; asking prices remain excluded. Retrospective reconstruction no longer requires proof a source was published before sale. Strict public-as-of replay still does.</p>
<div class="scroll"><table><tr><th>Candidate</th><th>Disposition</th><th>Reasons / qualifications</th></tr>{rows}</table></div>
<h2>2. Real holdout results</h2><table><tr><th>Holdout year</th><th>Sales</th><th>Within ±$50k</th><th>Median absolute error</th><th>Mean absolute error</th></tr>{headline(legacy)}{headline(initial_modern)}{headline(modern)}</table>
<p>2020: transactions explicitly marked LAND, filtered for repeat, deed and multi-parcel issues; historical acreage coverage was inadequate. 2024: sale-year vacant-land classification corroborated by prior-year vacant classification and zero building assessment. Blank transaction-type fields are allowed in this second cohort and remain a classification limitation. These are different cohorts, not an apples-to-apples improvement comparison. The two 2024 rows use the same test sales. The ±$50k policy chooses by 2022 hit rate only; it was added after seeing the initial MAE result and is a policy sensitivity, not a second independent validation.</p>
<p>Each model uses nonlinear gradient-boosted hedonic estimation. The 2024 model uses {e(', '.join(modern['model']['features']))}. Assessments are historical <em>predictors</em>; targets are actual county-reported sale prices. Area comes from WGS84 geodesic measurement of 2020 parcel polygons, not current parcel size. Missing and conflicting geometry stays unknown.</p>
<h2>3. 2024 results by closing price</h2><table><tr><th>Price band</th><th>Sales</th><th>Within ±$50k</th><th>Median absolute error</th></tr>{bands(modern)}</table>
<p>The overall ±$50k hit-rate interval is {pct(modern['within50000Wilson95'][0])}–{pct(modern['within50000Wilson95'][1])} (Wilson 95%; does not account for spatial dependence or selection bias).</p>
<h2>4. Same-sales baseline comparison, 2024</h2><table><tr><th>Estimator</th><th>Within ±$50k</th><th>Median absolute error</th><th>Mean absolute error</th></tr>{baselines(modern)}</table>
<p>The learned model beats these simple baselines on the 2024 cohort, but still misses the dollar band on most sales. A better baseline comparison is not evidence of adequate absolute accuracy.</p>
<h2>5. Validation controls</h2><ul><li>2024 partitions: {modern['partitions']['train']} training (2021), {modern['partitions']['tune']} tuning (2022), {modern['partitions']['calibration']} calibration (2023), {modern['partitions']['test']} untouched test targets (2024).</li><li>Repeated parcel identities are embargoed across partitions. Hyperparameters are chosen on tuning-period ±$50k hit rate (dollar error breaks ties); transforms and feature inclusion use training data only.</li><li>The 2024 holdout was evaluated after the first 2020 run identified historical area coverage problems. No 2024 outcomes were used to select features or hyperparameters; the separately reported tolerance-objective sensitivity was added afterward.</li><li>Point predictions do not require enough observations to calibrate intervals. The nominal 90% intervals covered {pct(modern['intervalCoverage'])} of 2024 sales, but had median width {money(modern['medianIntervalWidth'])}; wide coverage does not satisfy ±$50k.</li><li>No holdout records were deleted because their errors were large. Prices are not winsorized or replaced by estimates.</li><li>These are reconstructed historical tables retrieved today. Later source corrections and unknown original publication times prevent a strict point-in-time public-information claim.</li></ul>
<h2>6. Largest 2024 misses — retained in all metrics</h2><table><tr><th>County document</th><th>Actual price</th><th>Estimate</th><th>Signed error</th></tr>{cases(modern)}</table>
<h2>7. Capability checklist</h2><ul><li>Complete: audit all 17 rejections; recover supported research observations; distinguish admission from model support.</li><li>Complete: run actual public-data, chronological model backtests with dollar-band scoring, baselines, interval evaluation and per-sale audit.</li><li>Attempted but unresolved: demonstrate reliable ±$50k performance. Both tests fail to support that claim.</li><li>Blocked by data coverage: validate the Utah pilot. Three conditional historical land observations are insufficient for local multivariate fitting and independent testing.</li><li>Blocked by validation: publish a defensible automatic pilot market estimate. Existing dossier, easement/mineral/pool research and permit scope remain in the project ledger.</li></ul>
<h2>Sources and reproducibility</h2><p>Official <a href="https://github.com/ccao-data/wiki/blob/master/SOPs/Open-Data.md">county dataset documentation</a>; <a href="https://datacatalog.cookcountyil.gov/Property-Taxation/Assessor-Parcel-Sales/wvhk-k5uv">sales</a>; <a href="https://datacatalog.cookcountyil.gov/Property-Taxation/Assessor-Parcel-Universe-Historical-/nj4t-kc8j">historical parcels</a>; <a href="https://datacatalog.cookcountyil.gov/resource/uzyt-m557.json?$limit=1">assessments</a>; <a href="https://datacatalog.cookcountyil.gov/resource/2yvh-uwrw.json?$limit=1">2020 geometry</a>. Research scripts and versioned JSON inputs/results live in the repository. No paid feed or user-supplied closing records were used.</p></main></html>'''
(D/'valuation-audit.html').write_text(s)
print(D/'valuation-audit.html')
